1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,560
Everything falls.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:05,600
Raindrops, skydivers, and
right now the International

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,540
Space Station with
people on board.

4
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:09,380
Falling sounds simple.

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,640
You let go and you go down.

6
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,240
But look a little closer
and it gets strange.

7
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:16,800
There's a speed
you can't go past.

8
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:19,780
There's a famous record
that might not be true.

9
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:22,700
There's a piece of fabric
that turns a fall into

10
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:23,620
a soft landing.

11
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:26,060
And there's a space
station that's been falling

12
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:28,500
for more than 25 years
without ever hitting

13
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:29,300
the ground.

14
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:32,400
So let's find out what
actually happens when you

15
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:33,200
fall.

16
00:00:34,392 --> 00:00:37,312
Jump out of a plane, and
how fast do you end up

17
00:00:37,312 --> 00:00:38,112
going?

18
00:00:38,412 --> 00:00:41,072
You'd think the higher you
start, the faster you'll

19
00:00:41,072 --> 00:00:41,952
be by the end.

20
00:00:42,672 --> 00:00:43,932
But that's not how it works.

21
00:00:44,852 --> 00:00:47,732
When you first jump, gravity
speeds you up quickly.

22
00:00:48,572 --> 00:00:51,492
But the faster you go,
the harder the air pushes

23
00:00:51,492 --> 00:00:52,292
back.

24
00:00:52,592 --> 00:00:55,612
After about 12 seconds,
the push of the air matches

25
00:00:55,612 --> 00:00:58,812
the pull of gravity, and
you stop speeding up.

26
00:00:59,632 --> 00:01:02,812
That top speed is called
terminal velocity.

27
00:01:03,632 --> 00:01:06,532
Lying flat on your belly
the way most skydivers

28
00:01:06,532 --> 00:01:09,572
fall, it's about 200
kilometres an hour.

29
00:01:10,292 --> 00:01:12,832
Turn head down so you're
narrow like a dart,

30
00:01:13,292 --> 00:01:18,532
and there's less air in
the way, about 240 to 290.

31
00:01:18,532 --> 00:01:23,312
Speed skydivers push
this as far as it goes.

32
00:01:23,312 --> 00:01:26,432
They jump from high up,
where the air is thinner,

33
00:01:26,972 --> 00:01:29,592
tuck in tight, and
point straight down.

34
00:01:30,292 --> 00:01:34,332
In August 2025, Spain's
Sebastián García Gutiérrez

35
00:01:34,332 --> 00:01:39,212
set the official record,
an average of about 537

36
00:01:39,212 --> 00:01:42,012
kilometres an hour across
a whole competition.

37
00:01:42,812 --> 00:01:45,132
At the World Championships
this October, he went

38
00:01:45,132 --> 00:01:47,532
faster still, though
that's still waiting to be

39
00:01:47,532 --> 00:01:48,332
made official.

40
00:01:48,872 --> 00:01:51,272
So once you've been falling
for 10 seconds or so,

41
00:01:51,612 --> 00:01:54,012
falling further doesn't
make you any faster.

42
00:01:54,792 --> 00:01:56,972
The air sets your
speed, not the height.

43
00:01:58,804 --> 00:02:01,224
What's the highest fall
anyone has ever survived

44
00:02:01,224 --> 00:02:02,164
without a parachute?

45
00:02:02,924 --> 00:02:06,524
According to Guinness
World Records, it's 10,160

46
00:02:06,524 --> 00:02:08,004
metres.

47
00:02:08,924 --> 00:02:12,644
In January 1972, a Yugoslav
airliner broke apart

48
00:02:12,644 --> 00:02:15,104
in the sky over what
was then Czechoslovakia.

49
00:02:15,884 --> 00:02:17,904
The official investigation
blamed a bomb.

50
00:02:18,704 --> 00:02:21,144
Twenty-eight people were
on board, and twenty-seven

51
00:02:21,144 --> 00:02:21,944
of them died.

52
00:02:22,564 --> 00:02:25,044
The one survivor was a
22-year-old flight attendant

53
00:02:25,044 --> 00:02:26,384
called Vesna Vulović.

54
00:02:27,324 --> 00:02:29,524
A man from a nearby village
found her trapped in

55
00:02:29,524 --> 00:02:31,164
the wreckage, badly injured.

56
00:02:31,884 --> 00:02:34,404
She was in a coma for
three days, and it took her

57
00:02:34,404 --> 00:02:35,404
months to walk again.

58
00:02:36,104 --> 00:02:36,904
But she lived.

59
00:02:37,484 --> 00:02:39,644
And her fall, from the
plane's cruising height,

60
00:02:40,004 --> 00:02:41,204
went into the record books.

61
00:02:42,004 --> 00:02:46,344
Then, in 2009, two journalists
challenged that number.

62
00:02:47,064 --> 00:02:49,564
They argued the plane
actually broke up much lower,

63
00:02:50,104 --> 00:02:53,064
maybe only a few hundred
metres up, after a fast

64
00:02:53,064 --> 00:02:54,024
emergency descent.

65
00:02:54,804 --> 00:02:57,044
They pointed to how small an
area the wreckage covered,

66
00:02:57,544 --> 00:02:59,604
and to people on the ground
who saw it come down.

67
00:03:00,384 --> 00:03:02,244
They even suggested the
plane might have been shot

68
00:03:02,244 --> 00:03:04,824
down by mistake, and that
the high-altitude bomb

69
00:03:04,824 --> 00:03:06,604
story was invented to hide it.

70
00:03:07,044 --> 00:03:08,824
But one of the journalists
admitted the evidence

71
00:03:08,824 --> 00:03:11,964
was only circumstantial, and
it has never been proved.

72
00:03:12,544 --> 00:03:13,884
Guinness still lists the record.

73
00:03:14,684 --> 00:03:17,764
So was it 10 kilometres,
or a few hundred metres?

74
00:03:18,664 --> 00:03:21,204
Either way, she survived
something almost nobody

75
00:03:21,204 --> 00:03:22,004
survives.

76
00:03:22,684 --> 00:03:24,844
We just may never know
how far she really fell.

77
00:03:26,435 --> 00:03:29,475
How does a parachute turn
a 200 kilometre an hour

78
00:03:29,475 --> 00:03:31,475
fall into a gentle landing?

79
00:03:32,375 --> 00:03:33,335
It doesn't stop you.

80
00:03:33,995 --> 00:03:36,195
It gives the air something
much bigger to push against.

81
00:03:36,915 --> 00:03:38,455
Here's what happens, in order.

82
00:03:39,215 --> 00:03:42,135
At the right height, a
skydiver throws a small pilot

83
00:03:42,135 --> 00:03:43,055
chute into the wind.

84
00:03:43,855 --> 00:03:47,075
It catches the air, pulls
a pin, opens the container

85
00:03:47,075 --> 00:03:50,235
on their back, and drags
out a bag with the main

86
00:03:50,235 --> 00:03:51,695
parachute folded inside.

87
00:03:52,575 --> 00:03:55,635
The lines stretch out,
the bag lets go, and

88
00:03:55,635 --> 00:03:57,115
the canopy fills with air.

89
00:03:57,855 --> 00:04:00,655
A small square of fabric
called a slider sits on

90
00:04:00,655 --> 00:04:03,975
the lines and slows that
opening down, so it doesn't

91
00:04:03,975 --> 00:04:05,015
jolt you too hard.

92
00:04:05,755 --> 00:04:07,915
And modern canopies
aren't domes.

93
00:04:08,595 --> 00:04:11,935
They're wings, made of
cells that fill with air,

94
00:04:12,495 --> 00:04:16,975
so a skydiver can steer
and slow right down before

95
00:04:16,975 --> 00:04:17,835
touching the ground.

96
00:04:18,675 --> 00:04:20,775
Most of the time, it all works.

97
00:04:21,615 --> 00:04:24,295
But on roughly one jump
in a thousand, something

98
00:04:24,295 --> 00:04:25,095
goes wrong.

99
00:04:25,815 --> 00:04:26,855
The lines can twist.

100
00:04:27,715 --> 00:04:30,995
The canopy can open
lopsided, or get stuck in its

101
00:04:30,995 --> 00:04:31,795
bag.

102
00:04:32,135 --> 00:04:35,115
That's why every skydiver
carries two parachutes.

103
00:04:35,975 --> 00:04:38,875
If the main one fails,
they pull one handle to cut

104
00:04:38,875 --> 00:04:41,475
it away, and another
to open the reserve.

105
00:04:42,235 --> 00:04:45,195
In the US, that reserve has
to be checked and repacked

106
00:04:45,195 --> 00:04:49,195
by a certified rigger every
180 days, even if it's

107
00:04:49,195 --> 00:04:49,995
never used.

108
00:04:50,735 --> 00:04:54,055
And in case a skydiver
can't pull at all, many rigs

109
00:04:54,055 --> 00:04:56,755
carry a small computer
that keeps track of height

110
00:04:56,755 --> 00:04:57,555
and speed.

111
00:04:58,295 --> 00:05:00,275
If they're still falling
fast, too close to

112
00:05:00,275 --> 00:05:03,815
the ground, it fires and
opens the reserve by itself.

113
00:05:04,555 --> 00:05:07,235
So a parachute isn't one
clever piece of fabric.

114
00:05:08,095 --> 00:05:09,495
It's a chain of backups.

115
00:05:13,118 --> 00:05:15,798
The International Space
Station is falling right

116
00:05:15,798 --> 00:05:16,598
now.

117
00:05:16,758 --> 00:05:19,058
It has been for
more than 25 years.

118
00:05:19,958 --> 00:05:21,258
So why doesn't it
hit the ground?

119
00:05:22,098 --> 00:05:25,438
Up there, about 400
kilometres above us, gravity

120
00:05:25,438 --> 00:05:28,718
is still about 90% as
strong as it is down here.

121
00:05:29,238 --> 00:05:31,518
If the station just stopped,
it would come straight

122
00:05:31,518 --> 00:05:32,318
down.

123
00:05:32,498 --> 00:05:34,578
The trick is that it's
also moving sideways.

124
00:05:35,338 --> 00:05:36,138
Very fast.

125
00:05:37,038 --> 00:05:39,378
About 8 kilometres every second.

126
00:05:39,378 --> 00:05:41,178
Picture throwing a ball.

127
00:05:41,678 --> 00:05:43,538
It curves down and lands.

128
00:05:44,498 --> 00:05:46,898
Throw it harder, and
it lands further away.

129
00:05:47,678 --> 00:05:50,238
Throw it hard enough, and
the ground curves away

130
00:05:50,238 --> 00:05:52,438
beneath it as fast as it falls.

131
00:05:53,318 --> 00:05:55,398
It keeps falling,
and it keeps missing.

132
00:05:56,138 --> 00:05:56,938
That's an orbit.

133
00:05:57,378 --> 00:05:59,558
The station goes all the
way around the Earth about

134
00:05:59,558 --> 00:06:02,238
every 90 minutes,
16 times a day.

135
00:06:03,038 --> 00:06:05,558
And the astronauts float,
because they're falling

136
00:06:05,558 --> 00:06:09,618
too, at exactly the same
rate as the station around

137
00:06:09,618 --> 00:06:10,418
them.

138
00:06:10,678 --> 00:06:13,338
Even so, at that height,
there's still a tiny bit

139
00:06:13,338 --> 00:06:16,418
of air, and it slowly
drags the station lower.

140
00:06:17,098 --> 00:06:19,778
So every now and then,
an engine gives it a push

141
00:06:19,778 --> 00:06:20,938
to climb back up.

142
00:06:21,398 --> 00:06:25,338
Around 2030, NASA plans
to stop pushing and steer

143
00:06:25,338 --> 00:06:27,318
it down into a remote
part of the ocean.

144
00:06:28,724 --> 00:06:31,044
So that's what actually
happens when you fall.

145
00:06:31,824 --> 00:06:34,964
You speed up until the
air won't let you go any

146
00:06:34,964 --> 00:06:35,764
faster.

147
00:06:36,224 --> 00:06:38,904
A record can be harder to
pin down than it sounds.

148
00:06:39,824 --> 00:06:43,324
A few square metres of
fabric and a lot of backups

149
00:06:43,324 --> 00:06:45,544
can bring you down softly.

150
00:06:46,364 --> 00:06:49,344
And if you move sideways
fast enough, you can keep

151
00:06:49,344 --> 00:06:51,704
falling for decades
and never land.

152
00:06:52,544 --> 00:06:53,624
Everything is falling.

153
00:06:53,624 --> 00:06:56,504
Some things are just
very good at missing.
